PR #12 — Vercel Analytics and Speed Insights
Branch: feat/vercel-analytics           Depends on: #11             Status: DONE
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DESCRIPTION
  Page views and real-user Core Web Vitals. Speed Insights matters more than it
  looks: the LCP < 1.2s budget is currently only checked in CI on a runner, and
  this is how we find out whether it holds on a mid-range phone on real mobile
  data — which is what most of this product's traffic will be.

IMPLEMENTS
  [x] @vercel/analytics and @vercel/speed-insights in the root layout

EDGE CASES
  [x] Both are SAME-ORIGIN and cookieless. Verified in a real browser: the
      injected scripts are /_vercel/insights/script.js and
      /_vercel/speed-insights/script.js on our own host. That is precisely why
      these are acceptable and an ad stack is not — a visitor watching the
      Network tab to check their photo is not uploaded sees first-party
      requests, not a wall of third-party calls.
  [x] Neither can observe anything derived from the image; there is no API
      surface through which it could reach them.
  [x] Injected client-side on mount, so they add nothing to the server-rendered
      HTML and cannot delay first paint.
  [ ] The privacy page (#34) must state plainly that page-view and performance
      analytics exist, and that neither ever sees the photo. Claiming "nothing
      is collected" would be false.

PR #13 — PhotoSpec schema, Zod validation, registry (US, UK, Schengen)
Branch: feat/photospec-registry        Depends on: #12                Status: DONE
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DESCRIPTION
  The country requirement model. This registry is the moat; the schema decides
  how painful the next 37 countries will be.

IMPLEMENTS
  [x] PhotoSpec Zod schema with required provenance, plus a `verification`
      field — see below, it is the most important thing in this task
  [x] Registry keyed by country:document, parsed and frozen at module load
  [x] Schengen base spec authored as a RATIO, because that is the unit the
      standard publishes, plus a deep-merge for per-state overrides
  [x] Validation at registry construction: a malformed or duplicate spec throws
      before anything can serve it. `npm run validate:specs` in CI.
  [x] Staleness helper with an injected clock, so no test can go flaky by
      crossing the boundary on a particular day
  [x] findSpecIn takes its registry, keeping the lookup a pure function

  NEW CONCEPT — `verification: 'verified' | 'provisional'`
  A spec nobody has checked against the issuing authority's own page is
  PROVISIONAL and is not served. All four seeded specs are provisional: the
  values were authored from known figures, but the build environment cannot
  reach travel.state.gov or gov.uk, so nothing has been confirmed at source.
  Presenting an unverified government requirement as authoritative is the exact
  failure this product exists to prevent, so it is a state in the type system
  rather than a caveat in a comment.
  CONSEQUENCE: no country page will render until a human verifies a spec and
  flips the flag. That is intentional.

EDGE CASES
  [x] Inverted bands rejected: head height, eye line and digital edge sizes
  [x] Head height normalised once at the registry boundary and returned in BOTH
      units, with the authored unit preserved so a citation can quote what the
      authority actually published rather than our conversion of it
  [x] Deep merge for Schengen overrides. A shallow spread would replace the
      whole print or background object when a state overrides one field of it,
      silently dropping every sibling key — the spec would still parse, it
      would just be wrong.
  [x] Typed not-found that distinguishes an unknown country from an unsupported
      document, so the page can say the right thing
  [x] Deep-frozen at registry construction
  [x] Two documents in one country (us:passport and us:visa both seeded)
  [x] alternativePrintSizes for authorities accepting more than one
  [x] Source URL required by the schema; a spec without one fails to parse
  [x] Duplicate country:document pairs throw rather than silently overwriting

SEO
  [ ] lastVerified feeds sitemap lastmod and the visible "verified on" line

TESTS
  Unit: schema validation, merge logic, staleness, every seeded spec parses.
  Fixture: a deliberately invalid spec proves the build gate works.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #14 — Unit conversion and measurement utilities
Branch: feat/measurement-utils         Depends on: #13                Status: DONE
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DESCRIPTION
  Pure functions converting between mm, inches, pixels and DPI, and evaluating
  a measurement against a spec band. Small PR, disproportionate blast radius.

IMPLEMENTS
  [x] mm <-> inch <-> px conversions at a given DPI (moved into src/measurement)
  [x] Band evaluation returning status, signed delta AND a scale multiplier —
      the multiplier is what turns "21.4mm against 25.4mm" into "move about 19%
      closer to the camera", which is the product's core promise
  [x] Aspect-ratio helpers, including largestInscribedRect for the crop
  [x] Resolution sufficiency, which never suggests upscaling
  [x] Safe rounding policy: evaluate raw, round only for display

EDGE CASES
  [x] Rounding never turns a pass into a fail — evaluateBand never rounds the
      value it is given, and a 500-step sweep across the whole band asserts
      every value inside it is reported as within
  [x] Band widened by a tolerance far tighter than any published requirement,
      so sub-pixel landmark jitter cannot flip a boundary measurement between
      runs
  [x] Monotonicity asserted: a larger value never moves from above back within
  [x] Non-integer DPI; zero and negative dimensions; NaN and Infinity all
      rejected with typed errors rather than producing NaN downstream
  [x] Requirements beyond the browser canvas limit reported as such, rather
      than silently producing a blank bitmap
  [x] A crop is always proven to fit inside its source — a crop larger than the
      image produces the black band down one edge of a passport photo
  [x] Locale decimal separators, asserted against de-DE
  [x] Bands must have a non-negative minimum. Every band here is a physical
      quantity, so a negative edge is a bug, and requiring it removed a
      genuinely unreachable defensive branch rather than testing dead code.

SEO  n/a

TESTS
  Unit with property-based cases across the full plausible range.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #15 — Worker infrastructure, typed protocol and the fake-model test layer
Branch: feat/worker-infrastructure     Depends on: #14                Status: DONE
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DESCRIPTION
  The Web Worker plus its typed request/response protocol — and the fixture
  layer that lets every later PR reach 100% coverage without loading 5MB of
  WASM in a unit test. This is the mocking story for this product.

IMPLEMENTS
  [x] Worker entry point, bootstrap and a browser worker factory that
      feature-detects rather than assuming Worker exists
  [x] Discriminated-union message protocol, correlation IDs, typed both ways
  [x] Progress reporting, cancellation, timeout
  [x] Structured error propagation across the boundary — structured clone drops
      the prototype and stack, so errors are serialised to a branchable code
  [x] Detector interface so the real MediaPipe backend and a deterministic fake
      backend are interchangeable. createDetector() is the single seam PR #17
      replaces; until then it reports detector-unavailable rather than throwing
      at module load, which would misreport every request as worker-crashed
  [x] Fake detector deriving landmarks from each fixture's generation spec, so
      ground truth is exact by construction rather than measured
  [x] Fake worker running the real handler in memory, with failToStart,
      crashOnNextMessage and neverRespond to reach the failure paths
  [ ] DEFERRED to #16 — OffscreenCanvas bootstrap. It belongs with image
      ingestion, where there is something to decode and draw; adding it here
      would be untestable scaffolding with no caller.
  [ ] DEFERRED to #26 — Storybook decorator injecting the fake backend. There
      is no analysis UI to decorate yet. The Vitest side needs no setup: the
      fake is injected through createWorker, by construction.

EDGE CASES
  [x] Worker fails to start (CSP, blocked, private mode) => typed
      worker-unavailable, not a raw TypeError
  [x] Worker crashes mid-task => in-flight requests fail, listeners detached,
      the instance dropped, and the next call builds a fresh one
  [x] Cancellation arrives after completion => must not resolve twice. Every
      pending request settles exactly once, whatever path reaches it
  [x] Two analyses in flight; the stale one must be discarded, not rendered
  [x] A worker-reported cancellation settles the promise rather than leaving
      the caller waiting out the timeout
  [x] A cancellation mid-analysis stops the work at the next checkpoint rather
      than being noticed only at the end — on a phone that is seconds of
      blocked work for a screen the user has already left
  [ ] DEFERRED to #16 — out-of-memory on large images, structured-clone
      failures, and detached ArrayBuffers after transfer. All three are
      properties of the payload, and there is no image decoding yet to produce
      one. The error codes (out-of-memory) are already in the protocol.
  [ ] DEFERRED to #16 — Safari OffscreenCanvas feature detection, with the
      OffscreenCanvas work itself.

SEO
  [x] Worker created lazily on first use, never at module load, and referenced
      through new URL() so the bundler emits it as its own chunk — zero bytes
      on first paint

TESTS
  Unit: protocol, correlation, cancellation, timeout, crash recovery, the fake
  detector's own fidelity, and the fake worker's own contract. 100% coverage.
  [ ] DEFERRED to #26 — real worker round-trip in Playwright. It needs a page
      that runs an analysis, and the first one lands with the checker UI.

NOTE
  This PR also fixes CI, which had been red on every run since PR #2 and was
  never checked. The build refuses to start without an origin — deliberately,
  since a missing one produces relative canonicals and de-indexes the site —
  and the workflow supplied none, so every job after the build was skipped.
  Local builds passed only because .env.local is present and gitignored.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #15a — CI gate repair, and the accessibility defect it uncovered
Branch: feat/lighthouse-budgets         Depends on: #15               Status: DONE
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Unplanned, inserted rather than numbered, so the plan below keeps its
  numbering. PR #15 got the build running on CI for the first time since #2;
  the jobs behind it then failed for their own reasons, each one a gate that
  had never actually executed.

DESCRIPTION
  Three CI jobs were decorative. This makes them real, and fixes what they
  found the moment they ran.

IMPLEMENTS
  [x] @lhci/cli installed. The budgets job ran `lhci autorun` with the package
      never added, so it exited 127 every time.
  [x] lighthouserc.cjs with thresholds calibrated against a measured run —
      performance 1.00, LCP 0.5s, CLS 0, TBT 0ms — rather than guessed. A
      budget that fails on the day it lands teaches everyone to mute the job.
  [x] test:e2e builds Storybook first. The Playwright config serves
      storybook-static for every project, not just the stories one, so the app
      specs could never start on a clean checkout. CI's separate story step is
      now redundant and removed.
  [x] app/icon.svg. Chrome's automatic /favicon.ico request was 404ing on every
      page load and counting against best-practices.
  [x] eslint-plugin-react declared directly, and react/no-multi-comp scoped to
      *.tsx. Adding one .cjs file at the root took the whole lint suite down:
      the rule reached a file eslint-config-next's globs do not cover, where
      the borrowed plugin was unregistered.

  ACCESSIBILITY DEFECT (found by the gate, on its first real run)
  [x] --tk-text-tertiary was 4.34:1 on white — below WCAG AA. Lighthouse saw
      only that one, because it measures what the single page it visits
      happens to render. Measuring every pair found three more: status-warn at
      3.94:1 on sunken, status-pass at 4.40:1, status-manual at 4.49:1.
  [x] All four re-solved in HSL, hue and saturation preserved, darkened until
      each clears 4.5:1 against the darkest surface it can land on.
  [x] token-contrast.test.ts asserts every foreground token against every
      surface, in all three theme states — 72 pairs, independent of whether a
      component using them exists yet. Verified by reverting a token and
      watching it fail.
  [x] A separate assertion that the primary > secondary > tertiary hierarchy
      survives, so a future contrast fix cannot silently flatten it.

STILL OPEN — the visual suite is not yet reproducible
  [ ] --tk-font-mono is `ui-monospace, 'SF Mono', Menlo, monospace`, which
      resolves to whatever the machine has. Six of the sixteen baselines this
      PR regenerates changed only because the container was rebuilt, not
      because any design changed. Until the fonts are self-hosted, a green
      visual job proves the renderer matched itself, not that the UI is right.
      Owed its own PR.

TESTS
  Unit: contrast maths against published values, and the token invariant.
  All gates green locally: typecheck, lint, 698+ unit tests at 100% coverage,
  build, validate:specs, test:seo, 129 Playwright specs, lhci.

